// sumida's rule set — design §5.1 to §5.4.
//
// Read this file to find out who may do what. Every rule that used to live at a call site as an
// `if session.profile.is_admin` or a hand-built policy chain is here, which is the point: a rule in
// one place cannot be forgotten by the next caller.
//
// This is sumida's file and only sumida's. A product built on sumida keeps its own rules in its own
// repository and installs them at boot; nothing here knows what those are, and a family of resource
// that belongs to a product is added to a definition below with `extend`, from the product's file.
//
// Conventions:
//   · `on <table>.<col> [int|uuid]`  where a definition’s objects live, and what type its id is.
//                               Omitted when the objects are named by string alone (`service`,
//                               `platform`, `delegation_scope`).
//   · definition names use `_`  so a name is a valid Rust identifier and an unambiguous SQL
//                               literal. The *service object id* keeps today's colon form.
//   · `where` clauses are      column-qualified, because a derivation can be emitted inside a
//                               subquery where an unqualified name would bind to the wrong table.

// ─── terminal subject types ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

definition user   { }
definition apikey { }   // apikey:<blake3 hash>, the same hash `access_policies."user"` held as `api:<hash>`

// ─── R1 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Exactly one object: platform:root.
//
// `flagged` keeps `users.is_admin` as the operational source of truth, so the admin user screen is
// unchanged — while `admin` makes platform administration grantable without touching the `users`
// table, which today is only expressible as the `* | * | * | {*}` catch-all.
//
// The consequence that matters: the flag is now reachable only through the subject set, so a
// **secured API key bound to an admin no longer carries administrative power**. `who()` already got
// that right; the ~55 direct `session.profile.is_admin` reads got it wrong. Here there is nothing to
// get wrong, because there is no direct read on an authorization path.
definition platform {
  relation admin:   user | role#member
  relation flagged: user = column(users.id -> user where users.is_admin)

  permission superuser = admin | flagged
  permission grant     = superuser
}

// ─── the route-gate tier ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// resource_id is today's namespace string, colons included: `service:user`, `service:board_post`,
// and whatever a product declares for its own. `resource_type` and `resource_id` are separate
// columns, so the colons are inert — and the `/me/access-policies` compatibility payload stays
// byte-identical for the CASL client, which builds `can(action, `${namespace}:${object}`)`.
//
// One deliberate semantic change, stated plainly. The old evaluator ORed `object = '*'`
// unconditionally, so an `object='*'` row also satisfied the route gate. Here `op_read` grants the
// object-wide power *and* implies the route gate, but a route-gate grant (`read`) does **not**
// grant object-wide power. That is a narrowing, in the safe direction, and it is what makes "let
// Jiwon see everyone's records" a different grant from "let Jiwon use the records API".
definition service {
  // route-gate tier — what `service_ap!` checks (object = 'service' today)
  relation create: user | role#member | organization#member | apikey
  relation read:   user | role#member | organization#member | apikey
  relation update: user | role#member | organization#member | apikey
  relation delete: user | role#member | organization#member | apikey

  // object-wide tier — what `object = '*'` used to mean
  relation op_create: user | role#member | organization#member | apikey
  relation op_read:   user | role#member | organization#member | apikey
  relation op_update: user | role#member | organization#member | apikey
  relation op_delete: user | role#member | organization#member | apikey
  relation operator:  user | role#member | organization#member | apikey

  permission operate_create = op_create | operator | platform:root->superuser
  permission operate_read   = op_read   | operator | platform:root->superuser
  permission operate_update = op_update | operator | platform:root->superuser
  permission operate_delete = op_delete | operator | platform:root->superuser

  permission can_create = create | operate_create
  permission can_read   = read   | operate_read
  permission can_update = update | operate_update
  permission can_delete = delete | operate_delete

  permission grant = operator | platform:root->superuser
}

// The delegation tier: one service submitting work on behalf of its own users. The object id is
// compared for equality and by nothing else, which is the property that stops one service asserting
// another's scope — a prefix match here would be a cross-tenant hole that only shows up the day
// somebody registers a scope whose name starts with somebody else's.
definition delegation_scope {
  relation operator: user | role#member | apikey

  permission assert = operator | platform:root->superuser
  permission grant  = platform:root->superuser
}

// ─── R2: organizations, roles, users ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// `manager^` reads "a `manager` tuple on this organization **or on any ancestor of it**" — R2's
// direction: down, never up, never sideways. `manage` unions into every `manage_<family>`, so the
// single tuple `organization:100#manager@user:9` makes user 9 manager of everything in org 100 and
// everything beneath it. That one tuple is the whole of R2 for that person.
//
// `scope <family>` expands to three stored relations and three permissions (see `parse.rs`). Three
// tiers, not one, because the old model's per-action organization grants have to survive exactly:
// `{read}` is a viewer, `{read,update}` an editor, `{read,update,owner}` a manager.
//
// Only the families sumida itself has an organization-scoped resource for are declared here.
// Declaring an unused scope would let somebody grant a tuple that silently widens production — and
// a family that belongs to a product is the product's to declare, in its own file, with
// `extend organization { scope <family> }`. sumida cannot enumerate them and does not try.
definition organization on organizations.id int {
  closure parent via org_closure

  relation parent: organization = column(organizations.parent_id -> organization
                                         where organizations.parent_id IS NOT NULL)
  relation member: user         = column(users.id -> user of users.organization_id)

  relation manager: user | role#member | organization#member | apikey

  scope user   scope subscription  scope board  scope file

  // `credit` is written out rather than declared with `scope`, because it needs one extra branch.
  // `CreditService::authorize_use` (`membership/credit/mod.rs:88`) short-circuits on
  // `OwnerKind::Organization if session.profile.organization.id == owner_id` — a plain member of an
  // organization may spend that organization's credit. That is `member`, and `view_credit` has to
  // keep it or the migration takes a capability away from every ordinary account.
  relation credit_viewer:  user | role#member | organization#member | apikey
  relation credit_editor:  user | role#member | organization#member | apikey
  relation credit_manager: user | role#member | organization#member | apikey

  permission view_credit   = credit_viewer^ | edit_credit | member
  permission edit_credit   = credit_editor^ | manage_credit
  permission manage_credit = credit_manager^ | manage

  permission manage = manager^
  permission view   = member | manage | service:organization->operate_read
  permission edit   = manage | service:organization->operate_update
  permission remove = manage | service:organization->operate_delete
  permission grant  = manage
}

definition role on roles.id int {
  relation member: user = column(users.id -> user of users.role_id)
  relation admin:  user | role#member | organization#member

  permission view   = admin | service:role->operate_read
  permission edit   = admin | service:role->operate_update
  // `RoleService::delete` needs a permission of its own. `edit` would have done as a string and
  // that is exactly the G12 shape — deleting a role revokes every grant it carries, which is not
  // the same authority as renaming it.
  permission remove = admin | service:role->operate_delete
  permission grant  = admin | service:role->operate_update
}

// The *object* side of a user row. `user` stays the subject type; this is "may I act on that
// person". `UserService::update`'s `org:user` branch becomes `sumida_user:<id>#edit`, and the
// per-row recursive CTE it used is gone.
definition sumida_user on users.id int {
  relation org:     organization = column(users.organization_id -> organization)
  relation manager: user | role#member | organization#member | apikey

  permission view   = manager | org->manage_user | service:user->operate_read
  permission edit   = manager | org->manage_user | service:user->operate_update
  permission remove = manager | org->manage_user | service:user->operate_delete
  permission grant  = org->manage_user
}

// ─── boards ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

definition board_group on board_groups.id int {
  closure parent via board_group_closure

  relation parent: board_group = column(board_groups.parent_id -> board_group
                                        where board_groups.parent_id IS NOT NULL)

  relation owner:   user | role#member | organization#member | apikey
  relation editor:  user | role#member | organization#member | apikey
  relation viewer:  user | role#member | organization#member | apikey
  relation blocked: user | role#member | organization#member | apikey

  permission own    = owner^ | service:board_group->operate_delete
  permission edit   = (editor^ | own)  - blocked^
  permission view   = (viewer^ | edit) - blocked^
  permission create = edit
  permission grant  = own
}

// The `board_group` AND `board` composition is **preserved, not converted to a union**. It is what
// `board_post/mod.rs:406-411` (create) and `:739-744` (list) actually do, and the grammar admits
// intersection, so there is no reason to change the semantics. `editor_or_own_here` and
// `viewer_or_edit_here` are the local-only halves, named so the intersection reads as "the group
// permits it **and** this board permits it" — exactly how `ApChain` composes today.
//
// `org->manage_board` is dead when `boards.organization_id IS NULL`. That is how a public-board
// deployment opts out of organization scoping with no config flag; a public board is one tuple,
// `board:7#viewer@user:*`.
definition board on boards.id int {
  relation group: board_group  = column(boards.group_id -> board_group
                                        where boards.group_id IS NOT NULL)
  relation org:   organization = column(boards.organization_id -> organization
                                        where boards.organization_id IS NOT NULL)

  relation owner:   user | role#member | organization#member | apikey
  relation editor:  user | role#member | organization#member | apikey | user:*
  relation viewer:  user | role#member | organization#member | apikey | user:*
  relation blocked: user | role#member | organization#member | apikey

  permission editor_or_own_here = editor | own
  permission viewer_or_edit_here = viewer | edit

  permission own    = owner | org->manage_board | service:board->operate_delete
  permission edit   = (editor | own | (group->edit & editor_or_own_here))
                    - (blocked | group->blocked)
  permission view   = (viewer | edit | (group->view & viewer_or_edit_here))
                    - (blocked | group->blocked)
  permission create = edit
  permission grant  = own
}

definition board_topic on board_topics.id int {
  relation board: board = column(board_topics.board_id -> board)

  permission view   = board->view
  permission edit   = board->edit
  permission create = board->create
  permission remove = board->edit
  permission grant  = board->own
}

// `authorize_post_mutation` (`board_post/mod.rs:465-500`) transcribed:
//
//     allowed = (group_allow && board_allow) || owner_allow
//     denied  = group_deny  || board_deny   || owner_deny
//     result  = allowed && !denied
//
// `board->edit` already carries the `group & board` intersection, and the subtrahend is a union.
// The exclusion binds outside the whole union, so authorship cannot route around a block — and
// because it is in the rule rather than at a call site, the next caller cannot forget it.
//
// `author` derives from `board_posts.user_id`, which create already sets. The `ap::edit()` grant at
// `:448` and its matching revocation at `:613` become dead once this is live: ownership stops being
// a stored row that can drift from the column.
definition board_post on board_posts.id int {
  relation board:  board = column(board_posts.board_id -> board)
  relation author: user  = column(board_posts.user_id -> user
                                  where board_posts.user_id IS NOT NULL)
  relation blocked: user | role#member | organization#member | apikey

  permission view   = (author | board->view)
                    - (blocked | board->blocked | board->group->blocked)
  permission edit   = (author | board->edit)
                    - (blocked | board->blocked | board->group->blocked)
  permission remove = edit
  permission create = board->create
  permission grant  = board->own
}

definition board_file on board_files.file_uuid uuid {
  relation post: board_post = column(board_files.post_id -> board_post
                                     where board_files.post_id IS NOT NULL)
  relation file: file       = column(board_files.file_uuid -> file)

  permission view   = post->view | file->view
  permission create = post->edit
  permission remove = post->edit | file->remove
}

// `board_post_reaction` and `board_post_report` have a route gate and **no** row-level check
// anywhere today — an existing gap. Giving them definitions closes it, which is a widening of
// enforcement and is called out as such.
definition board_post_reaction on board_post_reactions.id int {
  relation post:   board_post = column(board_post_reactions.post_id -> board_post)
  relation author: user       = column(board_post_reactions.user_id -> user
                                       where board_post_reactions.user_id IS NOT NULL)

  permission view   = post->view
  permission create = post->view
  permission edit   = author
  permission remove = author | post->edit
}

definition board_post_report on board_post_reports.id int {
  relation post:   board_post = column(board_post_reports.post_id -> board_post)
  relation author: user       = column(board_post_reports.user_id -> user)

  permission view   = author | service:board_post_report->operate_read
  permission create = post->view
  permission remove = service:board_post_report->operate_delete
}

// The three permissions are distinct rules. sumida `67857ef` fixed `FileService::delete`
// authorising against `read`; here that mistake is no longer expressible, because `remove` and
// `view` are different permissions rather than different strings passed to `.action()`.
definition file on files.uuid uuid {
  relation owner: user         = column(files.user_id -> user where files.user_id IS NOT NULL)
  relation org:   organization = column(files.user_id -> organization via users.organization_id)
  relation viewer: user | role#member | organization#member | apikey | user:*

  permission view   = owner | viewer | org->manage_file | service:file->operate_read
  permission edit   = owner | org->manage_file | service:file->operate_update
  permission remove = owner | org->manage_file | service:file->operate_delete
  permission grant  = owner | org->manage_file
}

// ─── flat sumida resources ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// `ApiKeyService::create`'s "a non-admin may not issue a key for another user" guard
// (`api_key/mod.rs:92`, added by `67857ef`) becomes `sumida_user:<user_id>#edit` — issuing a key in
// somebody's name is exactly as powerful as editing them, and now says so.
definition api_key on api_keys.key {
  relation owner: user         = column(api_keys.user_id -> user)
  relation org:   organization = column(api_keys.user_id -> organization via users.organization_id)

  permission view   = owner | org->manage_user | service:api_key->operate_read
  permission edit   = owner | org->manage_user | service:api_key->operate_update
  permission remove = owner | org->manage_user | service:api_key->operate_delete
  permission grant  = owner | org->manage_user
}

definition ban on bans.id int {
  relation target: sumida_user = column(bans.user_id -> sumida_user where bans.user_id IS NOT NULL)
  relation author: user        = column(bans.created_by -> user where bans.created_by IS NOT NULL)

  permission view   = author | target->view | service:ban->operate_read
  permission edit   = target->edit | service:ban->operate_update
  permission remove = target->edit | service:ban->operate_delete
}

definition access_log on access_logs.id int {
  relation subject_user: sumida_user = column(access_logs.user_id -> sumida_user)

  permission view   = subject_user->view | service:access_log->operate_read
  permission remove = service:access_log->operate_delete
}

definition user_invitation on user_invitations.uuid uuid {
  relation invitee: sumida_user = column(user_invitations.user_id -> sumida_user)

  permission view   = invitee->view | service:user_invitation->operate_read
  permission edit   = invitee->edit | service:user_invitation->operate_update
  permission remove = invitee->edit | service:user_invitation->operate_delete
}

// `mail_form`, `membership_plan`, `role`, `organization` and `relation_tuple` are global
// configuration: no creator, no organization, so R2 and R3 do not apply and they are reachable only
// through R1 or an explicit grant. That is already the effective behaviour.
definition mail_form on mail_forms.kind {
  permission view   = service:mail_form->operate_read
  permission edit   = service:mail_form->operate_update
  permission remove = service:mail_form->operate_delete
}

definition membership_plan on membership_plans.id int {
  permission view   = service:membership_plan->operate_read
  permission edit   = service:membership_plan->operate_update
  permission remove = service:membership_plan->operate_delete
}

// The tuple table itself, replacing the `access_policy` namespace.
definition relation_tuple on relation_tuples.id int {
  permission view   = service:access_policy->operate_read
  permission create = service:access_policy->operate_create
  permission edit   = service:access_policy->operate_update
  permission remove = service:access_policy->operate_delete
}

// ─── the namespace inventory ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// sumida's own, enumerated from sumida's code, not from the design:
//   grep -rn 'service_ap!' --include='*.rs' api/src   → 18 namespaces × their actions
//   grep -rn 'ap::ns('     --include='*.rs' api/src   → the row-level namespaces below
//
// A product's namespaces are in the product's own rule file, and are not repeated here.
//
// `service` marks a route gate, so `service:<namespace>` is a real object; `= <definition>` says
// what row-level checks on the namespace resolve to. Validation rule 8 refuses to boot if any entry
// has no definition behind it, which is what makes "a namespace left out" impossible rather than
// merely unlikely.

namespace access_log          service = access_log
namespace access_policy       service = relation_tuple
namespace api_key             service = api_key
namespace ban                 service = ban
namespace board               service = board
namespace board_file          service = board_file
namespace board_group         service = board_group
namespace board_post          service = board_post
namespace board_post_reaction service = board_post_reaction
namespace board_post_report   service = board_post_report
namespace board_topic         service = board_topic
namespace file                service = file
namespace mail_form           service = mail_form
namespace membership_plan     service = membership_plan
namespace organization        service = organization
namespace role                service = role
namespace user                service = sumida_user
namespace user_invitation     service = user_invitation

// Row-level only — no `service_ap!` gate anywhere. `org:credit` and `org:subscription` keep a `use`
// tier distinct from `manage`: `credit/mod.rs:66` uses `manage`, `:93` uses `use`, and collapsing
// them would remove a capability that is in use. They map onto `organization`'s `credit` and
// `subscription` scopes.
namespace org:user         = sumida_user
namespace org:credit       = organization
namespace org:subscription = organization
